---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About"
  description="What TV Tattler is, how it is put together, and the reference-first approach behind it."
>
  <article class="page">
    <header class="page__header">
      <p class="mono page__kicker">About</p>
      <h1>Reference first, news second</h1>
      <p class="page__standfirst">
        TV Tattler is a soaps-first UK entertainment site built on evergreen reference
        depth — complete, accurate, well-maintained pages for every show, character and
        actor.
      </p>
    </header>

    <div class="page__body">
      <h2>What this is</h2>
      <p>
        One person writes here, a few times a week, so this site does not try to win at
        breaking news. Instead it does the thing the traffic-chasing newsrooms never keep
        up to date: it maintains the archive. Every show, every character, every actor gets
        a proper page — and those pages are the actual product. Articles keep things fresh;
        the reference pages are what people come back to.
      </p>

      <h2>How it is organised</h2>
      <p>
        Every article lives in exactly one section — <a href="/section/soaps/">Soaps</a>,
        <a href="/section/tv-and-film/">TV &amp; Film</a>,
        <a href="/section/celebrities/">Celebrities</a> or
        <a href="/section/news/">News</a> — so sections never compete with each other in
        search. Cross-cutting types of piece, like spoilers, cast changes and guides, get
        their own listings without becoming rival sections.
      </p>
      <p>
        Articles never link to each other. They point at entities — shows, characters and
        people — and all the cross-linking you see falls out of that automatically. An
        actor's roles are read straight from the portrayal records on the characters they
        have played, so nothing is maintained twice.
      </p>

      <h2>Accuracy</h2>
      <p>
        Reference pages are only worth keeping if they are right. Where a fact is uncertain
        it is checked before it goes in, and corrections are welcome. If you spot something
        wrong or out of date, please say so.
      </p>

      <h2>Where our information comes from</h2>
      <p>
        Our reference facts are our own, checked and written up here. Some open data
        helps keep the automatic pages fresh, and we credit it as those sources ask:
      </p>
      <ul class="credits">
        <li>
          Cast and show facts draw on <a href="https://www.wikidata.org" rel="external nofollow">Wikidata</a>
          (public domain), with any photos credited to their author and licence on the
          page they appear.
        </li>
        <li>
          Upcoming-episode air dates use data from
          <a href="https://www.tvmaze.com" rel="external nofollow">TVmaze</a> (CC&nbsp;BY-SA).
        </li>
        <li>
          Cinema-release listings use
          <a href="https://www.themoviedb.org" rel="external nofollow">The Movie Database (TMDB)</a>.
          This product uses the TMDB API but is not endorsed or certified by TMDB.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Contact</h2>
      <p>
        <!-- Placeholder address — replace with your real contact before launch. -->
        Corrections, tips and questions: <a href="mailto:editor@tvtattler.com">editor@tvtattler.com</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .page {
    max-width: 44rem;
    margin-inline: auto;
  }
  .page__header {
    border-bottom: 4px solid var(--live);
    padding-bottom: var(--space-4);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-5);
  }
  .page__kicker {
    color: var(--ink-40);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-2);
  }
  .page__standfirst {
    font-size: var(--step-1);
    color: var(--ink-80);
  }
  .page__body :global(h2) {
    font-size: var(--step-1);
    margin-top: var(--space-6);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-3);
  }
  .page__body p {
    max-width: var(--measure);
  }
  .page__body a {
    text-decoration-color: var(--live);
    text-decoration-thickness: 2px;
  }
</style>
